Undifferentiated Systemic Inflammatory Response
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Persistently elevated acute phase reactants (CRP/ESR) | Complete absence of fever or distinct infectious nidus | Vague constitutional symptoms only

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-043
Reason for Visit: Acute nocturnal exacerbation of severe whole-body pain, high fever, and extreme exhaustion, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude occult sepsis or acute endocarditis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute-onset, severe systemic inflammatory symptoms. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out evolving septic shock, systemic bacterial infection, or a catastrophic autoimmune flare, preventing severe hemodynamic collapse prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I am freezing cold but drenched in sweat, my bones hurt from the inside out, and I have absolutely no energy to even speak.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with profound, unexplained generalized malaise and an intermittent low-grade fever that spiked aggressively tonight. The patient reports severe, diffuse migratory arthralgias and fatigue completely out of proportion to exertion. There is pronounced intermittent nocturnal diaphoresis. The patient also notes unintentional mild weight loss over the past month and a mildly depressed mood with cognitive slowing.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound due to severe malaise and shivering.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver exhaustion and high anxiety regarding the severity of the symptoms.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 112/70 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 38.6 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient appears acutely unwell, flushed, and diaphoretic, experiencing mild rigors but alert and oriented. A review of past medical notes incidentally reveals a recent presentation for a dull pelvic ache with an absence of peritonitis and a completely non-diagnostic pelvic ultrasound; this symptom has fully resolved and is unrelated to the acute systemic inflammation tonight.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive systemic examination reveals a complete absence of localized infectious signs. Lungs are clear to auscultation. Cardiovascular exam reveals a sinus tachycardia without murmurs or peripheral stigmata of endocarditis. The abdomen is completely soft and non-tender. Neck is supple without meningismus. Generalized mild lymphadenopathy is palpable in the cervical and axillary chains (nodes are soft and mobile). Skin lacks any petechial or purpuric rash.
Environmental Observations: Room is extremely hot due to the patient utilizing a heavy electric blanket during the rigors.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking paracetamol with transient effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Undifferentiated Systemic Inflammatory Response - Status: Acute hypermetabolic flare.
Differentials:

Occult Bacterial Sepsis - Ruled out clinically for immediate shock risk due to stable blood pressure, alert mental status, and a complete lack of a focal infectious source (lungs, urine, skin clear), though requires urgent outpatient serology.

Infective Endocarditis - Ruled out due to the absence of new murmurs, splinter hemorrhages, Janeway lesions, or Osler nodes.

Lymphoma - Cannot be ruled out definitively without a biopsy; heavily suspected given the persistent B-symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss), but does not present an acute life-threat tonight.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's sudden, severe rigors and high fever mandated an immediate after-hours evaluation due to the critical risk of evolving septic shock. Telephone triage accurately identified the potential for rapid hemodynamic collapse. A meticulous, head-to-toe physical examination confirmed absolute hemodynamic stability, a clear sensorium, and the complete absence of a localized bacterial nidus. By firmly establishing that the patient was experiencing a stable, albeit severe, systemic inflammatory flare rather than acute decompensating sepsis, the clinician successfully avoided an unnecessary emergency department admission, implementing immediate physiological cooling and securing an urgent outpatient diagnostic pathway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered weight-based oral NSAIDs for potent antipyretic and anti-inflammatory effect. The expected investigation results include an elevated C-reactive protein and erythrocyte sedimentation rate without a specific focal infectious source, which will assist in confirming the severe inflammatory state via comprehensive urgent blood work.
Positive Behavioral Change: To break the cycle of shivering and sweating, immediately turn off the electric blanket and use only a single, thin cotton sheet; physically lowering your microclimate temperature will directly suppress the brain's fever response and stop the exhausting rigors.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24 hours for a comprehensive infectious, autoimmune, and malignant serology panel.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient becomes confused, develops a stiff neck, experiences a rash that does not fade when pressed, or if their blood pressure drops leading to severe dizziness when sitting up.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; severe systemic symptoms with B-symptoms (sweats/weight loss) mandate exhaustive, urgent outpatient investigation.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves continuing, unpredictable spikes of severe fever and exhaustion until the underlying etiology (autoimmune, infectious, or hematologic) is formally diagnosed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if you develop any difficulty breathing, a severe new headache, or if you completely stop passing urine for more than 8 hours.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: We are not administering antibiotics tonight because blindly treating a fever without a clear source can dangerously mask a serious underlying condition and delay the exact blood tests you urgently need tomorrow.
Actionable Steps: Take your anti-inflammatory medication strictly every 6 hours around the clock, even if you feel okay, to aggressively suppress the fever spikes and prevent another severe episode of shivering.

